"Not All Test-Time Compute Is Evidence:Model-Specific Bias in LLM Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1)\nciteturn41233view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23213v1\",\"lineno\":285}); Total lines: 472\nL236: ##### Equal-budget summaries.\nL237: \nL239: Table 3: Direct trace comparison with full traces or equal-budget summaries (Qwen3-8B base model). Final Pref. is the fraction of reasoning-stage disagreements where the reviewer ensemble selects the final-stage prediction. Sel. Acc. is the correct-selection rate when exactly one candidate is correct. The last two columns report the probability of selecting the intermediate-stage prediction when only it is correct, and selecting the final-stage prediction when only it is correct, respectively.\nL240: Setting  | Dataset  | Final Pref.  | Sel. Acc.$\\uparrow$  | Inter.$\\checkmark$ / Final$\\times\\uparrow$  | Inter.$\\times$ / Final$\\checkmark\\uparrow$\nL241: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL242: Full Trace  | MuSR  | 93.0%  | 47.1%  | 5.6%  | 93.8%\nL243: MMLU-Pro  | 91.8%  | 73.2%  | 26.7%  | 100.0%\nL244: Equal Budget  | MuSR  | 69.8%  | 50.0%  | 33.3%  | 68.8%\nL245: MMLU-Pro  | 61.7%  | 65.0%  | 57.1%  | 69.2%\nL246: #### 4.3.2 Homogeneous Qwen3-8B Reviewers\nL247: \nL248: We replace the heterogeneous solvers with three independently sampled Qwen3-8B solvers. The base model remains Qwen3-8B, and the arbitration rule is unchanged. Both settings are evaluated on the same activated reasoning-stage disagreements.\nL249: Arbitration accuracy decreases on all three datasets, with drops of more than 30% on both 2WikiMultiHopQA and MuSiQue (Table cite93†4 ). On these two datasets, every question answered correctly by the homogeneous panel is also answered correctly by the heterogeneous panel. This pattern is consistent with the model-specific error bias discussed in Section cite7†2 . Takeaway. It supports using heterogeneous solvers to obtain corrective evidence rather than relying on repeated samples from the base model.\nL250: #### 4.3.3 Scaling to Stronger Base Models\nL251: We keep the three heterogeneous solvers fixed and evaluate Qwen3 base models at 8B, 30B, and 80B scales^{1}^{1} 1 The 30B and 80B variants are Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 and Qwen3-Next-80B-A3B-Instruct, respectively. on MuSR and MMLU-Pro. Dia outperforms both CoT and DEER in all six settings (Table cite93†4 ). The gains persist at 80B without upgrading the external solvers. Takeaway.\nL252: Together with the homogeneous-solver ablation in Section cite30†4.3.2 , these results suggest that heterogeneous models provide useful complementary predictions even as the base model becomes stronger.\nL253: Table 4: Solver heterogeneity and base-model scaling. Left: Effect of solver heterogeneity with Qwen3-8B as the base model. Arbitration accuracy is measured on activated reasoning-stage disagreements; the homogeneous panel uses three independently sampled Qwen3-8B solvers. Right: Scaling the base model with a fixed heterogeneous solver panel. We report full-dataset accuracy and activation rate, defined as the fraction of examples that trigger arbitration through reasoning-stage disagreement.\nL254: Dataset  | Hetero.  | 3 Qwen3-8B  | $\\Delta$\nL255: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL256: MuSR  | 55.81%  | 51.16%  | $-4.65\\%$\nL257: 2WikiMultiHopQA  | 53.85%  | 19.23%  | $-34.62\\%$\nL258: MuSiQue  | 54.72%  | 24.53%  | $-30.19\\%$\nL259: Dataset  | Base Model  | Accuracy (%) $\\uparrow$  | Activation (%)\nL260: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL261: CoT  | DEER  | Dia\nL262: --- | --- | ---\nL263: MuSR  | Qwen3-8B  | 60.00  | 61.00  | 63.50  | 21.50\nL264: Qwen3-30B  | 62.00  | 61.50  | 66.00  | 24.50\nL265: Qwen3-80B  | 66.00  | 67.50  | 68.50  | 14.50\nL266: MMLU-Pro  | Qwen3-8B  | 71.43  | 68.00  | 75.14  | 17.43\nL267: Qwen3-30B  | 75.14  | 72.86  | 76.00  | 13.43\nL268: Qwen3-80B  | 76.86  | 75.71  | 78.00  | 9.14\nL269: Activation rates decrease with model scale, as larger models form more stable intermediate-stage predictions, yet Dia continues to improve accuracy while invoking external solvers less often.\nL270: ## 5 Related Work\nL271: ##### Systematic error bias in LLM reasoning.\nL272: The errors of a language model are not independent across samples, reasoning steps, or self-evaluation. At the generation level, models commit to an answer early and then rationalize it (cite68†Zhang et al., 2024 ), larger models misfire at the commitment step despite retaining the correct answer (cite94†Yeom et al., 2026 ), and this commitment is skewed by surface features and prior beliefs (cite95†Zheng et al., 2024 ; cite96†Jiang et al., 2024 ; cite97†Dasgupta et al., 2022 ).\nL273: Because such biases are shared across samples, majority voting over repeated samples (cite55†Wang et al., 2023 ) can amplify a shared wrong answer rather than average it out.\nL274: At the reasoning level, chain-of-thought explanations can be unfaithful to the factors that drive the prediction (cite98†Turpin et al., 2023 ), reasoning models form strong preliminary answer preferences that trigger overthinking (cite66†Dang et al., 2026 ; cite99†Chen et al., 2025 ), and longer reasoning does not reliably improve accuracy (cite67†Zhou et al., 2026 ).\nL275: At the evaluation level, LLM judges exhibit position, verbosity, and self-preference biases (cite69†Zheng et al., 2023 ; cite100†Wataoka et al., 2024 ; cite101†Ye et al., 2025 ; cite70†Hu et al., 2025 ). Prior work studies these biases in isolation, largely on multiple-choice or short chain-of-thought settings; we show that they co-occur within a single reasoning model and address them at test time by acquiring evidence from outside the model.\nL276: ##### Adaptive reasoning and early exit.\nL277: Test-time compute can be allocated adaptively (cite62†Snell et al., 2025 ), either by constraining reasoning length (cite102†Han et al., 2025 ; cite103†Xu et al., 2025 ; cite104†Aggarwal and Welleck, 2025 ) or by monitoring intermediate answers and stopping once they stabilize or become confident (cite57†Fu et al., 2025 ; cite56†Yang et al., 2026 ; cite58†Dai et al., 2025 ).\nL278: These methods decide how much to compute and trust the intermediate-stage prediction whenever it is confident, which is where the preliminary-answer bias above resides. Dia uses the same kind of signal to decide when to seek external evidence, and retains both the intermediate-stage and final-stage predictions.\nL279: ##### Multi-agent reasoning and its failure modes.\nL280: Multi-agent methods improve reasoning by letting agents exchange and revise answers (cite59†Wu et al., 2024 ; cite60†Li et al., 2023 ; cite105†Liang et al., 2024 ), by layering models on earlier outputs (cite72†Wang et al., 2025 ), or by sampling more agents and voting (cite61†Li et al., 2024 ).\nL281: Recent analyses question whether communication adds independent evidence: agents conform to a majority, especially when uncertain, and frequently switch from a correct answer to an incorrect one to match their peers (cite106†Zhu et al., 2025 ; cite107†Cho et al., 2025 ; cite108†Wynn et al., 2025 ); most of the benefit of debate comes from majority voting rather than from the exchange itself (cite73†Smit et al., 2024 ; cite109†Choi et al., 2025 ); and many failures of agent systems trace to inter-agent misalignment and weak verification (cite110†Cemri et al., 2025 ).\nL282: Cost is a further concern, since invoking a full agent system on every query is expensive, which motivates query-dependent agent architectures (cite111†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite112†Zhang et al., 2025 ).\nL283: Dia is designed around these findings: solvers reason independently and never see the base model’s candidates or each other, their answers are aggregated directly rather than through a candidate-aware judge, which inherits the judge biases above (Table cite92†3 ), the panel is heterogeneous because homogeneous solvers share the base model’s error modes (Section cite30†4.3.2 ), and it is invoked only on reasoning-stage disagreements.\nL284: ## 6 Conclusion\nL285: We show that model-specific error bias persists across repeated sampling, continued reasoning, and self-evaluation, so that more compute from the same model does not amount to more evidence. Dia uses reasoning-stage disagreement to trigger candidate-blind solving by heterogeneous models and aggregates their answers directly. Across seven benchmarks, it outperforms CoT in 12 of 14 settings and achieves higher average accuracy with fewer tokens than the multi-agent baselines.\nL286: Ablations show that asking heterogeneous models to compare the base model’s reasoning traces inherits its final-stage preference, and that replacing the heterogeneous panel with repeated samples of the base model sharply reduces arbitration accuracy, so independent solving and solver heterogeneity are both necessary. The gains persist as the base model scales to 80B while the external solvers remain fixed.\nL287: Test-time scaling should therefore consider where additional evidence comes from and how it is combined, not only how much computation is used.\nL288: ## Ethics Statement\nL289: \nL290: We confirm that all authors have read and adhered to the ICLR Code of Ethics. This work complies with the ethical principles outlined therein, and no part of this study violates ethical standards.\nL291: ## References\nL292:   * Aggarwal and Welleck (2025) P. Aggarwal and S. Welleck L1: controlling how long a reasoning model thinks with reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.04697. Cited by: cite113†§5 .\nL293:   * Cemri et al. (2025) M. Cemri, M. Z. Pan, S. Yang, L. A. Agrawal, B. Chopra, R. Tiwari, K. Keutzer, A. Parameswaran, D. Klein, K. Ramchandran, M. Zaharia, J. E. Gonzalez, and I. Stoica Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fai